  "articleBody": "When it comes to handrail installation, it's easy to make a few common mistakes that can compromise safety and functionality. One big one is installing rails that are loose or shaky. A handrail needs to be securely anchored to provide proper support, so always confirm safe mounting points. Another frequent error is choosing a rail that's hard to grip. The profile and size of the handrail should be comfortable for the user to hold, not just look good. Installing rails that are too short or positioned incorrectly, without matching the actual run of the stairs or ramp, is another common oversight. The rail should extend far enough to provide continuous support. Lastly, many people treat the handrail as merely decorative rather than load-bearing safety hardware. It's crucial that the rail is constructed to safely support someone's weight and assist with balance. To avoid these issues, especially when anchoring into masonry, concrete, or aging wood, or when needing to comply with local codes, consider bringing in a qualified installer to get it right.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}